---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const listenCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Comprehensible input (Krashen)', note: 'Acquisition happens when you understand messages slightly beyond your level — "i+1". Grammar, vocabulary and fluency all grow fastest through massive meaningful exposure, not rule study alone (the linguistics ladder\'s acquisition lesson has the evidence).' },
    { math: '2 · Choose the difficulty band', note: 'Target content you understand ~80–90% of: graded readers, children\'s shows, podcasts for learners. Too hard = noise; too easy = stagnation. The band moves as you grow — keep recalibrating.' },
    { math: '3 · Volume beats intensity', note: 'An hour daily of understandable content beats a weekly cram. Input is the compound interest of language learning (the finance lesson\'s logic, applied to hours).' },
    { math: '4 · Intensive vs extensive listening', note: 'Intensive: one clip, replayed until every word is caught (builds precision). Extensive: hours of easier content (builds fluency and intuition). The mix: extensive for volume, intensive for precision.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/listening-comprehensible-input">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Listening is <strong>the highest-volume input channel and the one learners most
    undertrain</strong>. The method: massive exposure to content you understand mostly —
    comprehensible input (Krashen's "i+1") — which drives acquisition of vocabulary, grammar and
    intuition simultaneously, without a single rule being explained.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="i+1, the difficulty band, volume, and the two listening modes" widget="equationStep" config={listenCfg} />

  <h2>Practical sources at each level</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beginner:</strong> graded readers with audio, learner podcasts (slow speed, limited vocabulary), children's cartoons — deliberately simple, deliberately short.</li>
    <li><strong>Intermediate:</strong> native podcasts on familiar topics, TV with target-language subtitles, YouTube on your existing hobbies (you know the content, so you can infer the language).</li>
    <li><strong>Advanced:</strong> films without subtitles, news, comedy — humour and cultural reference are the final milestones, because they depend on inference and shared knowledge.</li>
    <li><strong>The subtitle question, answered:</strong> target-language subtitles aid vocabulary and reading bridge; native-language subtitles turn it into reading practice. Progression: native subs → target subs → none.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Krashen, Principles and Practice (1982); Nation, What Should Every EFL Teacher Know?; Rogers & d\'Arcy on extensive listening research.</p>
</TopicPage>
